     "id": "Realization",
     "label": "Realization",
     "predicate": "realizes",
     "predicateLabel": "Realizes",
     "predicateDefinition": "Indicates that an entity plays a critical role in the creation, achievement, sustenance,\nor operation of a more abstract entity.",
     "notation": "r",
     "parent": "",
     "definition": "A realization relationship represents that an element plays a critical role in the creation, achievement, sustenance, or operation of a more abstract element.",
     "scopeNote": "A realization relationship indicates that more abstract elements (“what” or “logical”) are realized by means of more tangible elements (“how” or “physical”). The realization relationship is used to model run-time realization; for example, that a process realizes a service, and that a data object realizes a business object, an artifact realizes an application component, or a core element realizes a motivation element.\nThe interpretation of a realization relationship is that the whole or part of the source element realizes the whole of the target element (see also Section 5.1, “Structural Relationships”). For example, this means that if two internal behavior elements have a realization relationship to the same service, either of them can realize the complete service. If both internal behavior elements are needed to realize, the grouping element or an And-junction (see :Junction) can be used. For weaker types of effects on the realization of a motivation element, the influence relationship (see Section 5.2.3, “Influence Relationship”) should be used.\nFor realization, nesting as a notation is allowed in both directions of the relationship. For instance, a service can be nested in an application component to express that it is realized by that application component, and a process nested in a capability may express that this process realizes the capability. Since this can lead to ambiguity, it is advisable to use it only in one direction in the same view and add an explanation to any view in which such ambiguity may arise.\n A “Transaction Processing” function realizes a “Billing Service”; the “Invoice” business object is realized by the data object “Billing Data”.",
     "changeNote": "4.0: Cross-layer realization between services of different domains replaced by specialization (from realizing service to realized service) or aggregation (from realized service to realizing service).",
